WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.801
-Our next guest
is a very funny comedian

00:00:02.801 --> 00:00:04.534
whose memoir, "Foolish:
Tales of Assimilation,

00:00:04.534 --> 00:00:06.367
Determination, and Humiliation"
is available now.

00:00:06.367 --> 00:00:08.968
Please welcome back to the show
Sarah Cooper, everybody!

00:00:08.968 --> 00:00:10.634
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:10.634 --> 00:00:17.901
♪♪

00:00:17.901 --> 00:00:25.200
♪♪

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:27.968
Welcome to the show
in person this time!

00:00:27.968 --> 00:00:29.901
-I'm in the seat.
-Last time you were here,

00:00:29.901 --> 00:00:31.734
we talked about
your very popular videos

00:00:31.734 --> 00:00:34.234
where you lip-sync Donald Trump.
-Who?

00:00:34.234 --> 00:00:37.501
-Yeah. I'm wondering, because of
how many of those you did,

00:00:37.501 --> 00:00:40.567
which one of us you think is
more sick of hearing his voice?

00:00:40.567 --> 00:00:43.634
Me or you?
-Um, probably you.

00:00:43.634 --> 00:00:45.234
-See, I think it's you
because I --

00:00:45.234 --> 00:00:47.868
At least when I do his voice, I
get to make him say what I want.

00:00:47.868 --> 00:00:49.467
-Yeah, yeah.
-Where you were, like,

00:00:49.467 --> 00:00:51.934
locked in to, like,
actual things he said.

00:00:51.934 --> 00:00:54.133
-Right. It's more terrifying.
-Yeah.

00:00:54.133 --> 00:00:56.367
-Right. Yeah. It's, uh --
[ Laughs ]

00:00:56.367 --> 00:00:58.567
The things you're saying
are actually kind of funny.

00:00:58.567 --> 00:01:01.033
The things he's saying is --
You are scared.

00:01:01.033 --> 00:01:02.767
-Yeah.
-You're just scared. Yeah.

00:01:02.767 --> 00:01:04.334
-This is --
This is a wonderful book.

00:01:04.334 --> 00:01:06.167
-[ Gasps ] Thank you.
-You're very welcome.

00:01:06.167 --> 00:01:08.834
And a memoir. We actually --
We were talking --

00:01:08.834 --> 00:01:11.434
We were at a dinner,
and we were talking
about writing a memoir.

00:01:11.434 --> 00:01:12.868
And you actually
have some advice

00:01:12.868 --> 00:01:15.434
to anyone who would take this
leap into writing a memoir?

00:01:15.434 --> 00:01:18.634
-Yes. So, the one thing
I wish I had done

00:01:18.634 --> 00:01:21.968
before writing this book
is read a book.

00:01:21.968 --> 00:01:25.701
-Yeah. That's a really smart,
like, step one.

00:01:25.701 --> 00:01:27.767
-It's just -- I wish
I had just picked one up.

00:01:27.767 --> 00:01:30.567
But, see, the thing is, I skim.
-Yeah, you're a skimmer.

00:01:30.567 --> 00:01:32.534
-I'm a skimmer.
I don't really read.

00:01:32.534 --> 00:01:35.000
Who reads? Does anybody read?
You just buy books.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:39.000
You don't actually read them.
So that was my first mistake.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:41.601
And I do want to apologize
to the gentleman

00:01:41.601 --> 00:01:43.734
who reviewed the book
on Goodreads

00:01:43.734 --> 00:01:47.400
who said, "Sarah admitted that
she wrote most of this high,

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:48.901
and it shows."

00:01:48.901 --> 00:01:50.467
Um...
[ Laughter ]

00:01:50.467 --> 00:01:52.267
-That seems like
a positive review.

00:01:52.267 --> 00:01:54.067
He's calling you
a truthful person.

00:01:54.067 --> 00:01:56.133
-I am, but should I have
written in the book

00:01:56.133 --> 00:01:57.501
that I wrote this book high?

00:01:57.501 --> 00:01:59.534
Should I have done that?
I don't know.

00:01:59.534 --> 00:02:03.834
But I think if you skim it
while high it's really good.

00:02:03.834 --> 00:02:05.534
It's a really good book.
It's a really good book.

00:02:05.534 --> 00:02:07.067
-That's a very interesting way
of saying it.

00:02:07.067 --> 00:02:10.033
"Look. I was high. Don't beat
yourself up. Just skim it."

00:02:10.033 --> 00:02:13.767
-You know, write high,
edit sober, submit drunk.

00:02:13.767 --> 00:02:17.100
-There you go. You talk about
your comedy being very --

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:18.501
[ Applause ]
Yes. This is --

00:02:18.501 --> 00:02:21.501
This is good memoir advice.

00:02:21.501 --> 00:02:23.801
You talk about how your comedy
is very popular with dads.

00:02:23.801 --> 00:02:25.400
Could you extrapolate on that?

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:29.801
-Dads love me.
They love me. They love me.

00:02:29.801 --> 00:02:32.334
Too bad they're married.
-Yeah. Not all of them.

00:02:32.334 --> 00:02:34.467
-Not all of them.
Not all of them.

00:02:34.467 --> 00:02:37.033
But, yeah,
the first time I got recognized,

00:02:37.033 --> 00:02:39.467
I was walking
my little dog, Stella.

00:02:39.467 --> 00:02:42.667
She's a cute little Cockapoo.
And she was taking a giant poop.

00:02:42.667 --> 00:02:45.367
And I had my mask on,

00:02:45.367 --> 00:02:48.000
and so I bent down
to pick up the poop,

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:50.267
but I couldn't get
the little green bag open,

00:02:50.267 --> 00:02:54.300
so I took down my mask to lick
my fingers to open the bag.

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:56.834
Very sanitary.

00:02:56.834 --> 00:02:59.968
And this little girl,
10-year-old girl,

00:02:59.968 --> 00:03:04.300
came over and she said,
"Are you Sarah Cooper?"

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:07.534
And I was like, "It's happening.
It's happening."

00:03:07.534 --> 00:03:10.534
And I was like,
"Yes, I'm Sarah Cooper.

00:03:10.534 --> 00:03:12.868
Are you on TikTok?"
[ Laughs ]

00:03:12.868 --> 00:03:16.434
And she was like, "No.
My dad sends me your videos."

00:03:16.434 --> 00:03:18.901
[ Laughter ]

00:03:18.901 --> 00:03:20.667
Yeah.
-Heartbreaking.

00:03:20.667 --> 00:03:23.267
-My dog saw the whole thing.
I mean...

00:03:23.267 --> 00:03:25.133
-Yeah.
-It was really embarrassing.

00:03:25.133 --> 00:03:26.934
And at first,
I was like, you know,

00:03:26.934 --> 00:03:28.801
maybe he was just kind of,
you know,

00:03:28.801 --> 00:03:30.701
a younger
sort of millennial dad.

00:03:30.701 --> 00:03:32.567
You know, not that bad.
-Sure. Yeah.

00:03:32.567 --> 00:03:34.634
-But then I was doing
my Netflix special,

00:03:34.634 --> 00:03:36.801
and Marisa Tomei came over to me
and she said,

00:03:36.801 --> 00:03:38.701
"Sarah, can I get
a picture with you?!"

00:03:38.701 --> 00:03:41.534
And I was like, "Oh, my God.
Marisa Tomei wants
a picture with me?!"

00:03:41.534 --> 00:03:43.300
And she was like,
"No, it's for my dad."

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:45.133
-Oh, yeah...
That's how you know.

00:03:45.133 --> 00:03:47.167
Once you get
that second example...

00:03:47.167 --> 00:03:49.367
-But hello to Marisa Tomei
and her dad.

00:03:49.367 --> 00:03:50.667
-And her dad.
-Yes.

00:03:50.667 --> 00:03:53.100
-You talk about
how you are dating again.

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:55.300
-Yeah.
-You write about your divorce

00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:57.267
very openly and honestly.
-Yes.

00:03:57.267 --> 00:03:59.901
-And you're dating again
and that men are....

00:03:59.901 --> 00:04:01.601
You find men to be robots

00:04:01.601 --> 00:04:03.300
currently in your
dating experiences,

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:05.000
and you have some red flags.
-I do.

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:07.434
-Okay. What are some red flags
we should look out for?

00:04:07.434 --> 00:04:09.567
-Tall with a good head of hair.

00:04:09.567 --> 00:04:11.100
-Bad? That's bad?

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:14.334
-See, Elon Musk
is building these robots

00:04:14.334 --> 00:04:16.334
because he needs, you know --
he needs little androids

00:04:16.334 --> 00:04:18.567
to build his Teslas.
-Yeah.

00:04:18.567 --> 00:04:19.868
-And so he knows

00:04:19.868 --> 00:04:23.133
that a woman will overlook
just about anything

00:04:23.133 --> 00:04:25.100
if a man is tall
with a good head of hair.

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:26.567
-So that is likely a robot.

00:04:26.567 --> 00:04:28.701
-That is likely a robot.
-Okay. Interesting.

00:04:28.701 --> 00:04:32.000
-Short bald men are okay.
-Okay. Gotcha.

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:33.567
-They're okay.
-Yeah.

00:04:33.567 --> 00:04:36.968
-Um, let's see. Monotone voice.
-Yeah.

00:04:36.968 --> 00:04:40.067
-Okay? So if he uses
the same tone of voice

00:04:40.067 --> 00:04:42.067
to say, "I went to Duke,"

00:04:42.067 --> 00:04:45.300
as he does to say,
"I'm gonna [bleep]," that's...

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:46.501
-Yeah.

00:04:46.501 --> 00:04:48.200
-That's a robot.
-That's a robot.

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:50.000
-That's a robot.
Watch out for that.

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:52.100
-The funny thing
is he doesn't seem excited

00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:53.701
about either of those things.

00:04:53.701 --> 00:04:55.267
-Either of those things.
It's a red flag.

00:04:55.267 --> 00:04:57.033
-Yeah.

00:04:57.033 --> 00:05:00.834
-The other one is if he responds
to really cute text messages

00:05:00.834 --> 00:05:02.334
in a weird way.

00:05:02.334 --> 00:05:04.801
Like, if you wake up
thinking about him

00:05:04.801 --> 00:05:06.601
and you send him a text message
and you're like,

00:05:06.601 --> 00:05:08.100
"I woke up thinking about you,"

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:09.667
and he responds, "LOL."

00:05:09.667 --> 00:05:11.434
Um...

00:05:11.434 --> 00:05:13.400
-Classic robot tell.
-Classic robot.

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:15.534
-So helpful.
This is also very helpful.

00:05:15.534 --> 00:05:18.300
You're doing a very kind thing
with your wedding ring.

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:21.000
You're divorced.
You're gonna auction it off.

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:23.033
-I am.
-For a good cause.

00:05:23.033 --> 00:05:24.734
-Yes.
-What is the cause?

00:05:24.734 --> 00:05:27.334
-I'm such a good person!
Um...

00:05:27.334 --> 00:05:30.133
I, um -- I'm giving it to the
Entertainment Community Fund...

00:05:30.133 --> 00:05:32.133
-That's very nice.
-...to auction it off.

00:05:32.133 --> 00:05:33.968
-This is helpful
during strike times.

00:05:33.968 --> 00:05:35.834
-Yes, during strike times.

00:05:35.834 --> 00:05:39.000
Because I -- I wanted
to be part of the WGA

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:41.267
for so long, the Writers Guild.

00:05:41.267 --> 00:05:43.868
And in 2020,
I was actually able to join

00:05:43.868 --> 00:05:47.400
and get my health insurance
through the WGA.

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:49.000
And without
that health insurance,

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:50.934
I wouldn't have been able
to get divorced.

00:05:50.934 --> 00:05:52.100
-That's fantastic!

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:53.467
-So I'm donating
my wedding ring.

00:05:53.467 --> 00:05:55.067
-And you can go
to your Instagram page.

00:05:55.067 --> 00:05:57.033
-Yes. Go to my Instagram.
-Sarah Cooper, everybody.

00:05:57.033 --> 00:05:58.834
"Foolish" is available
wherever you buy books,

00:05:58.834 --> 00:06:00.934
but please support local
and independent book stores.

00:06:00.934 --> 00:06:02.634
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

